# the K4510 Linux, LIVE, the package list.  Diverges from k4510.list.chroot in four
# ways, each of them a consequence of Doc's 2026-09-03 requirements:
#
#   + networking      there was NONE in the original list -- no DHCP client,
#                     no NetworkManager, nothing.  The t520 image would have
#                     come up with no way to reach anything.  The machine's
#                     TELNET needs the outside world to dial BBSes.
#   + iwlwifi         the T480's Intel 8265.  Absent from the original, which
#                     was built for a wired thin client.
#   + Mad Pascal deps FPC was already here; git and the checkouts do the rest.
#   - installer       nothing can be installed: the internal drives are
#                     blacklisted out of existence, so the launcher would only
#                     ever show an empty list.

# --- the machine ---
libsdl2-2.0-0
alsa-utils

# --- the toolchain, installed by default (Doc, 2026-09-02) ---
# K4510 is the hosted build with the desktop taken away and the compilers
# kept.  cc65 builds the ROM; 64tass rebuilds Forth; nasm the Tube; FPC is
# what rebuilds Mad Pascal's mp AND the MADS assembler beside it.
build-essential
libsdl2-dev
cc65
acme
64tass
nasm
fp-compiler
fp-units-fcl
python3
git
make
unzip
patch

# --- the keyboard ---
# A dead-key console layout so the stick types accents like the desktop (Doc,
# 2026-09-10).  console-setup turns /etc/default/keyboard into a kernel keymap
# with dead keys; SDL's console keyboard composes accents from it.  kbd brings
# loadkeys/setupcon.  Without this the console is a plain US map: no accents.
console-setup
kbd

# --- somewhere to work ---
# neovim by name (Doc, 2026-09-03).  vim stays: the machine's own VI is a
# different thing entirely, and muscle memory for `vi` should land somewhere.
neovim
vim
less
ca-certificates

# --- the network ---
# NetworkManager because the answer to "how do I get on the wifi" has to be a
# thing you can do from a text console with no notes: Ctrl+Alt+F2, `sudo
# nmtui`, pick the SSID.  Ethernet needs no ceremony -- DHCP just runs.
network-manager
wpasupplicant
iproute2
iputils-ping
telnet
inetutils-telnetd
# ftp://, sftp://, scp:// go through curl and ssh on the host (Doc, 2026-09-10);
# curl's Debian build has the SSH protocols, openssh-client brings ssh/sftp.
curl
openssh-client

# --- the hardware ---
# KMSDRM is the ONLY video path here, so the GPU firmware is not optional:
# without it there is no KMS, and that appears as a black screen and an SDL
# failure rather than as anything naming a driver.  The T480 is Intel (UHD
# 620); the AMD blob stays so the same image still boots the t520.
firmware-iwlwifi
firmware-misc-nonfree
firmware-intel-graphics   # trixie moved i915's firmware here: kbl_dmc (the Dell's display power states, 2026-09-12)
firmware-linux-free
firmware-amd-graphics

# CHESS.PRG's engine, on the Tube (program 5)
stockfish

# The boot splash: the K4510 logo while the machine starts (Plymouth, with the
# theme in config/includes.chroot/usr/share/plymouth/themes/k4510; Doc,
# 2026-09-12: "a cool splash logo")
plymouth
plymouth-themes
# Wi-Fi's regulatory database: the kernel asked for regulatory.db at every
# boot, and without it the radio keeps to the world-safe channels
wireless-regdb
# pam_systemd: its absence was "PAM unable to dlopen(pam_systemd.so)" at every
# login (user@.service stays masked in the layer; nothing needs a user manager)
libpam-systemd
# initramfs-tools is set to COMPRESS=zstd, and without the tool it quietly
# falls back: the 2026-09-13 rebuild's initrd came out 90 MB, twice the last,
# and no longer fit the image.  Named here, not left to a dependency.
zstd
